---
- hosts: all
  vars:
    TYPE: wireplumber-profile
    INSTANCE: main
    ETC_FILES:
      - name: wireplumber-profile.conf
        content: |
          monitor.alsa.rules = [
            {
              matches = [
                {
                  # WEILIANG 24BIT USB DAC (usb 1-3) shares controller
                  # 0000:02:00.0 with the BS5P-ARC (usb 1-2). Both are
                  # full-speed iso devices; together they exhaust the
                  # controller's isochronous bandwidth, the WEILIANG capture
                  # interface fails usb_set_interface with -ENOSPC every ~5s,
                  # and the erroring node wedges the whole combine_sink driver
                  # group (total silence despite RUNNING states). Pin the card
                  # playback-only so the capture interface never opens.
                  # Restore duplex only after moving it to another controller.
                  device.name = "alsa_card.usb-WEIL_WEILIANG_24BIT_USB-01"
                }
              ]
              actions = {
                update-props = {
                  device.profile = "output:analog-stereo"
                }
              }
            }
          ]
      - name: wireplumber-rules.conf
        content: |
          # Revived from the pre-0.5 flat files ~/.config/wireplumber/
          # 60-line-in.conf and 61-hdmi-delay.conf, which WirePlumber 0.4+
          # never loaded (flat files outside wireplumber.conf.d/ are
          # ignored). Nothing in them was active: 60-line-in.conf was fully
          # commented scratch, 61-hdmi-delay.conf was empty.
          #
          # 2026-10-16 REGRESSION LESSON: the first revival of this file
          # shipped the 60-line-in rule as a real monitor.alsa.rules block
          # whose actions.update-props was EMPTY, matching the PCI line-in
          # node (the node the whole graph elects as master driver). After
          # the next wireplumber restart every sink kept state "running"
          # but emitted digital silence; pulse monitor taps returned zero
          # bytes. Removing the drop-in restored audio immediately. A rule
          # that matches a node must never carry an empty update-props;
          # when there is nothing live to set, ship documentation only and
          # link nothing into wireplumber.conf.d/. This file is therefore
          # deliberately not installed (see install-user.sh) and defines
          # no configuration keys.
          #
          # Intent inventory:
          # - 60-line-in: the live line-in -> speakers plumbing is
          #   pw-loopback.service (compfuzor pw-loopback.srv.pb), capturing
          #   alsa_input.pci-0000_0b_00.4.analog-stereo into combine_sink.
          #   Original scratch props, never active: node.description,
          #   channelVolume (":q" was never valid syntax).
          # - 61-hdmi-delay: per-port latency offsets are route state in
          #   ~/.local/state/wireplumber/default-routes, edited via
          #   pavucontrol's per-port "Latency offset" field (live value at
          #   capture: 57.5ms on alsa_card.pci-0000_07_00.1
          #   hdmi-output-2). A drop-in cannot set port offsets.
      - name: AUDIO-NOTES.md
        content: |
          # Audio volume layering on this host — non-active notes

          Generated by compfuzor `wireplumber-profile.etc.pb`.
          Documentation only: nothing in this file changes behavior.
          Captured 2026-10-15 by opencode session ses_ebe73f371ffeY6VbgQe6OrhOX9
          after the total-silence incident (USB isochronous bandwidth
          exhaustion on the WEILIANG DAC + wedged PCI HD-Audio capture
          driver stalling the whole graph; see wireplumber-profile.conf for
          the codified half of that fix).

          ## The stack, end to end

          Four volume layers multiply. Signal path for an app (e.g. Firefox):

              app stream        (pavucontrol "Playback" tab; pactl set-sink-input-volume)
            x combine_sink      (default sink; "Output Devices" green-check row)
            x hardware sink     (per-device row in "Output Devices"; pactl set-sink-volume)
            x device/amp knob   (physical)

          Example with 2026-10-15 levels: Firefox 100% x combine 45%
          x BS5P 71% = ~32% effective at that DAC (reads ~ -30 dB on
          pavucontrol's cubic-dB scale; the displayed per-slider dB values
          use a cubic curve and do not sum linearly).

          ## Why it is nested on purpose

          The combine sink fans one stream to every DAC. Keeping per-device
          levels separate is the point: each speaker chain gets its own trim
          while the combined slider (and each app) scales everything
          together. The operator explicitly wants per-device adjustment
          independent of the combined level. Do not "fix" this by
          flattening.

          ## Where things live

          - combine sink + fan-out: compfuzor `pipewire-virtual-sink.etc.pb`
            (~/.config/pipewire/pipewire.conf.d/virtual-sink.conf)
          - line-in -> combine loopback: compfuzor `pw-loopback.srv.pb`
            (systemctl --user cat pw-loopback)
          - WEILIANG output-only pin: `wireplumber-profile.conf` (this
            subsystem)
          - volume/route/profile persistence incl. per-port latency
            offsets: ~/.local/state/wireplumber/{default-routes,default-profile}
          - structural caveat: the permanent line-in loopback chains the
            PCI capture into the playback driver group, so one wedged
            capture device can silence all output (suspend/resume is the
            known trigger). Recovery: stop pw-loopback, kill pavucontrol,
            suspend the source, unbind/rebind snd_hda_intel for
            0000:0b:00.4, restart pw-loopback.

          ## Incident log

          - 2026-10-16: total digital silence after a wireplumber restart,
            zero system events overnight. Cause: wireplumber-rules.conf
            revival shipped an EMPTY update-props rule on the PCI line-in
            node (the graph's elected driver). Every sink stayed "running"
            while emitting silence; monitor taps returned zero bytes.
            Removed the drop-in -> audio returned. Rules matching a node
            must not carry empty update-props; see wireplumber-rules.conf.
          - 2026-10-15: total silence. Causes: WEILIANG DAC isochronous
            bandwidth exhaustion (-ENOSPC sharing controller 0000:02:00.0
            with BS5P-ARC; codified output-only pin in
            wireplumber-profile.conf) + wedged PCI HD-Audio capture
            stalling the whole driver group. Fixed by unplug/profile-pin
            and snd_hda_intel unbind/rebind on 0000:0b:00.4.

          ## Non-active example: codifying default device levels

          Intentionally NOT active — runtime state owns device levels today
          (and state restore runs after monitor rules, so it would win
          anyway). If ever wanted, a monitor.alsa.rules update-props on each
          sink's node.name can seed channel volumes:

          # monitor.alsa.rules = [{
          #   matches = [{ node.name = "alsa_output.usb-Generic_BS5P-ARC_20170726905955-00.analog-stereo" }]
          #   actions = { update-props = {
          #     channelVolumes = [ 0.71 0.71 ]
          #   }}
          # }]
    BINS:
      - name: install-user.sh
        exec: |
          dest="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d"
          mkdir -p $dest
          ln -sf ${ETC}/wireplumber-profile.conf $dest/
          # wireplumber-rules.conf is documentation-only (see its header);
          # deliberately NOT linked into wireplumber.conf.d/.
    ENV:
      ETC: "{{ETC}}"
      APP: wireplumber
  tasks:
    - import_tasks: tasks/compfuzor.includes
